(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-allergy-inflammation-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Allergy, Inflammation & Dry Eye Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 2","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How does the technique for a gel or ointment differ from a drop?","choices":["A ribbon into the pocket, with no duct occlusion","The lacrimal duct must be covered for twice as long","The eye should be held open rather than closed","It should be applied directly to the cornea rather than the pocket"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The process is otherwise the same, but ointment does not drain away like a solution, so the punctal occlusion step is unnecessary. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A patient has used an over-the-counter redness-relieving eye drop daily for a month. On stopping it, the eye becomes redder than before. What has happened?","choices":["A new allergic conjunctivitis unmasked by stopping the drop","Tachyphylaxis, meaning the drug no longer works while it is being taken","Preservative toxicity from long-term use","Rebound hyperaemia, from downregulation of alpha-1 receptors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. These are alpha-adrenergic agonists that vasoconstrict to reduce redness and conjunctival oedema. Constant activation downregulates the receptors, so when the drug stops there are fewer left for endogenous noradrenaline and adrenaline to act on, and the vessels dilate. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Besides drug therapy, which physical interventions are offered for dry eye?","choices":["Warm compresses and lid massage only","Contact lens wear to protect the surface","Punctal plugs and surgical occlusion","Intravitreal injection of tear substitute"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The same trick used to boost drug absorption is used here for tears themselves — keep what little there is on the eye rather than letting it drain. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What are the ocular indications for a topical nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory, and what is it NOT recommended for?","choices":["Bacterial conjunctivitis, to reduce discomfort while antibiotics work","Chronic dry eye with inflammation","Fungal keratitis, to limit scarring","Postoperative inflammation and allergic conjunctivitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Their place is after surgery and in allergic disease; they are explicitly not routinely recommended for conjunctivitis generally. Their adverse effects include lacrimation, keratitis and a rise in intraocular pressure. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which adverse effects limit ocular glucocorticoid use, and how long is a course generally kept to?","choices":["Only transient burning, so courses may run for months","Rebound hyperaemia on withdrawal, limited to under two weeks","Cataract, raised pressure, infection and delayed healing","Permanent iris colour change, limited to a single dose"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The pressure rise is greater with a family history. Because steroids suppress local immune function they also enable infection, which is why topical steroid use appears on the fungal keratitis risk list. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A patient is being taught to instil an eye drop. What is named as the single most important step?","choices":["Tilting the head back to form a lower lid pocket","Washing the hands thoroughly with soap and water","Holding the eyes closed for two to three minutes","Covering the lacrimal duct after instilling"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is marked as the most important step, and it comes before anything else. The dropper must then not touch anything, so it stays sterile. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Besides suppressing inflammation, what other effect of glucocorticoids is useful in the eye?","choices":["They increase aqueous outflow, lowering pressure","They promote corneal epithelial healing","They have antimicrobial activity against surface organisms","They inhibit fibrin and collagen deposition, reducing scar formation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In a structure where a scar in the wrong place costs vision, limiting collagen deposition is a benefit in its own right — and it is part of why they are used after surgery. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"How do ocular mast cell stabilisers work?","choices":["They inactivate the H1 receptor as inverse agonists","They inhibit mast cell degranulation","They inhibit phospholipase A2","They block cyclooxygenase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. They stop the mediators being released at all, and also dampen release from basophils, eosinophils and neutrophils. That is prevention rather than blockade — and it is why they cannot rescue an acute attack. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which adverse effect of ocular nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs matters most in a patient who also has glaucoma?","choices":["Increased tear production","Rebound hyperaemia","Cataract formation","A rise in intraocular pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lacrimation, keratitis and irritation are the nuisance effects; the pressure rise is the one that interacts with an existing diagnosis and could undo glaucoma control. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"How long may an over-the-counter ocular vasoconstrictor be used, and what should the patient be told if there is no improvement?","choices":["Under two weeks; see a provider if no better in 72 hours","Up to three months, with review if symptoms persist","Indefinitely, as they are available without prescription","Less than two weeks, but no review is needed if symptoms persist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Short-term use only, because of rebound hyperaemia. And the 72-hour rule exists because a red eye that is not improving may be something more serious than allergy. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which three agents are listed as mast cell stabilisers?","choices":["Olopatadine, epinastine and azelastine","Cromolyn, lodoxamide and nedocromil","Ketorolac, nepafenac and diclofenac","Naphazoline, tetrahydrozoline and pheniramine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A short list worth holding because everything else in the allergy section is an antihistamine, a vasoconstrictor or an anti-inflammatory. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A patient has acute itchy, watery eyes from a sudden pollen exposure and wants relief today. Which class is appropriate, and why?","choices":["A mast cell stabiliser, because it prevents histamine release","A topical glucocorticoid, because it suppresses the allergic response","An antihistamine, because the onset is within minutes","An ocular nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory, because it blocks prostaglandins"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antihistamines work within minutes, which is what an acute presentation needs. They are also typically preferred over mast cell stabilisers, though two weeks of therapy is needed to judge full efficacy. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slides 44 and 47"},{"q":"By what mechanism do ocular glucocorticoids suppress the late-phase allergic reaction?","choices":["They inhibit phospholipase A2","They block cyclooxygenase only","They inhibit interleukin-2 production","They stabilise mast cell membranes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cutting off arachidonic acid stops the whole downstream cascade, not just the prostaglandin arm. They also inhibit fibrin and collagen deposition, which is why they reduce scar formation. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"After placing a drop, the patient is told to close the eyes and press a finger over the lacrimal duct. What does that achieve?","choices":["It limits drainage and systemic absorption","It prevents the drop being diluted by reflex tearing","It spreads the drug evenly across the cornea","It reduces the stinging on instillation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Occluding the punctum stops the drop draining down the nasolacrimal duct, so more stays on the eye and less reaches the systemic circulation — the same principle as silicone plugs, done by hand for two to three minutes. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A patient starts an ocular antihistamine. How long before they feel relief, and how long before its full effect can be judged?","choices":["Minutes for relief, two weeks for full efficacy","Five to fourteen days before any effect at all","Full effect immediately, with no further change","Six weeks, as with most topical ocular therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both halves matter in counselling — the drug does something immediately, so it will not feel useless, but the final verdict takes a fortnight. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which mediators are released in the ocular allergic response, and what do they produce?","choices":["Interleukin-2 and interferon gamma, producing scarring","Histamine, platelet-activating factor and leukotrienes","Prostaglandins only, producing pain","Immunoglobulin E itself, producing direct vasodilation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Those four signs are what every drug in this section is aimed at, and knowing which mediator each class addresses is what sorts antihistamines from stabilisers from steroids. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"How does the vasoconstrictor component of an over-the-counter allergy drop reduce redness?","choices":["It blocks histamine at the H1 receptor","It stabilises mast cells to prevent degranulation","It activates alpha-adrenergic receptors on vessels","It inhibits cyclooxygenase in the conjunctiva"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Constricting those vessels reduces conjunctival oedema and the visible redness. It treats the appearance rather than the allergy, which is why it is paired with an antihistamine in products such as Naphcon-A. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 48"}]};
